## Four systems, two contrasts {#sec-casys-design}

| System | Tillage | Nitrogen input |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SD1 | Permanent no-till | — |
| SD2 | Non-permanent no-till | — |
| TS1 | Tilled | Exogenous nitrogen |
| TS2 | Tilled | None |

The systems sit in three zones: a no-till zone, a tilled zone, and a mixed zone where both occur. That spatial arrangement is deliberate, and it is the reason the tillage contrast is not a clean comparison. The consequence is on [the practice contrast and where it sits](#sec-casys-confounding).

::: {.notes}
The zone structure is the key design fact to plant now. It pays off twice: in the design block, where it is a blocking question, and in the causal block, where it is confounding by construction. Do not yet give the numbers.
:::

## The zones are the design {#sec-casys-zones}

![Three zones: no-till with SD1 and SD2, mixed with both practices, and tilled with TS1 plus nitrogen and TS2 without it.](figures/generated/casys-design-sketch.jpg){fig-alt="Three sketched zones. The no-till zone holds SD1 and SD2. The mixed zone holds a no-till field and a tilled field. The tilled zone holds TS1 with nitrogen and TS2 without it." width="92%"}

SD1 and SD2 are the no-till systems. TS1 and TS2 are tilled, and only TS1 adds exogenous nitrogen. The mixed zone is where both practices occur.

::: {.notes}
Use the picture to fix the two contrasts: tillage runs left to right, nitrogen is only the TS1 label. The mixed panel is why a tillage coefficient is not a clean comparison. One minute, then the code.
