## A table of counts is a log-linear model {#sec-loglinear}

[Penn State STAT 504, Lesson 10](https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat504/Lesson10) models a contingency table by a Poisson GLM. The cell count is $Y_{ab} \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_{ab})$. The lesson is this log-linear model. Independence, with neither margin as the response, is

$$
\log \mu_{ab} = \alpha + \alpha_a + \alpha_b.
$$

::: {.notes}
$Y_{ab}\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_{ab})$; independence $\log\mu_{ab}=\alpha+\alpha_a+\alpha_b$. PSU STAT 504 lesson—joint model, not logit of one margin.
:::
## Independence, drawn

```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 10
%%| fig-height: 2.2
flowchart LR
  a["level $$a$$"] --> eta["$$\log\mu_{ab}=\alpha+\alpha_a+\alpha_b$$"]
  b["level $$b$$"] --> eta
  eta --> y["$$Y_{ab}\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_{ab})$$"]
```

The fitted counts are row total times column total over $n$, and every odds ratio equals 1. A logit model is the conditional distribution of one margin. This log-linear model is the joint distribution. Dummy coding changes the separate coefficients. Differences of coefficients, the log odds, stay put.

::: {.notes}
A logit-equivalent log-linear model includes every association among the predictors. The lesson contrasts R's dummy coding, first level set to 0, with the zero-sum constraint in `loglin()`. Expected counts do not depend on that choice.
:::

## A normal latent on the log mean {.fit-fig #sec-pln}

![The Poisson log-normal model: counts and covariates enter a latent Gaussian layer, the counts are Poisson given the latent, and the same layer is constrained for a known grouping, a mixture, a low-rank covariance, or a sparse precision.](figures/sources/poisson-log-normal.webp){fig-alt="Standard Poisson log-normal. Counts Y and covariates X enter a latent Z drawn normal with mean mu and covariance Sigma. Given Z, the counts are Poisson with mean exp of Z. The mean of Z is X times a coefficient matrix. Extensions constrain that mean for known classes or unknown groups, and constrain Sigma to low rank or to a sparse precision." width="96%"}

[Chiquet, Mariadassou, and Robin](https://doi.org/10.3389/fevo.2021.588292). The figure's $\mu$ is the mean of the latent Gaussian. Here that mean is $m$, and $\mu$ stays $\mathbb{E}(Y \mid x)$.

::: {.notes}
Chiquet figure: latent $Z$, Poisson on $\exp(Z)$, extensions constrain $\mathbf{m}$ or $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$. Figure $\mu$ is latent mean $m$; slide $\mu$ stays $\mathbb{E}(Y\mid x)$.
:::
## The Poisson log-normal drawing

$$
\mathbf{Z}_i \sim \mathrm{Normal}(\mathbf{m}_i, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}),
\quad
m_{i\ell} = \mathbf{x}_i^{\top}\boldsymbol{\theta}_\ell,
\quad
Y_{i\ell}\mid Z_{i\ell} \sim \mathrm{Poisson}\!\left(\exp(o_{i\ell}+Z_{i\ell})\right).
$$

Townes: replace the gamma prior on the rate by a log-normal. The normal is the distribution of $\log$ of the Poisson mean.

::: {.notes}
Read $\mathbf{Z}_i\sim\mathrm{Normal}(\mathbf{m}_i,\boldsymbol{\Sigma})$ and Poisson on $\exp(o+Z)$. Townes: log-normal latent replaces gamma for rate.
:::
## The moments sit above the Poisson line {#sec-pln-moments}

Aitchison and Ho, as quoted by Chiquet, Mariadassou, and Robin, give the margin after integrating the latent:

$$
\mathbb{E}(Y_{i\ell}) = \exp\!\left(o_{i\ell} + m_{i\ell} + \sigma_{\ell\ell}/2\right),
$$

$$
\mathrm{Var}(Y_{i\ell}) = \mathbb{E}(Y_{i\ell}) + \mathbb{E}(Y_{i\ell})^2\left(e^{\sigma_{\ell\ell}}-1\right).
$$

The variance exceeds the mean. The covariance of two features has the sign of $\sigma_{\ell\ell'}$. Given $\mathbf{Z}_i$, the features are independent, so the dependence lives in $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$. Twice the effort adds $\log 2$ to the offset $o_{i\ell}$.

::: {.notes}
The likelihood has no closed form once $\mathbf{Z}$ is integrated out. The paper fits it by a variational approximation, in the R package `PLNmodels`. The four corners of the figure constrain either $\mathbf{m}$ (a known class, or a mixture) or $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ (low rank, or a sparse precision).
:::

## What these three models do not cover

- a zero-inflated count is the [mixture](#sec-count-zip) already named, and it sits outside the exponential family
- a composition is the [conditioning on the library size](#sec-multinomial-sum); a log-ratio, after a deliberate treatment of the zeros, is a further model
- a continuous flux: often a positive continuous distribution, or a Gaussian model on a scale you can defend
- dependence in space or in time: the design block is next

Quasi-Poisson is a variance fix, not a full likelihood. Use it only if you can say what the interval means.

::: {.notes}
Bullet list: ZI mixture, composition conditioning, continuous flux, space-time design next. Quasi-Poisson variance fix only if you can interpret the interval—closing honesty slide.
:::


## Smooth $\mu(x)$, and say what you assumed {#sec-smooth}

StatHarbor Analytics, *Smoothing and Nonparametric Methods*, uses "nonparametric" for a curve that is not fixed in advance as a short formula. Complexity may grow with $n$. The structure that replaces the formula is smoothness, locality, or a penalty.

::: assumption
$$
Y = \mu(x) + \varepsilon, \qquad \mathbb{E}(\varepsilon \mid x) = 0.
$$
The course notes write $f(x)$ for this $\mu(x)$. A straight line $\mu(x) = \mathbf{x}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\beta}$ is efficient when it is close to the curve, and biased when the curve bends.
:::

[Angelino and Marcone](https://www.sib.swiss/training/course/20260907_ADDMG) make the same opening: a linear model is easy to interpret, and the straight line is often an approximation.

::: {.notes}
Start in the assumption callout: $\mu(x)$ is the object, not a transform of $Y$. Point at the sentence about the straight line being efficient only when the truth is nearly linear. A common slip is to treat "nonparametric" as "no assumptions"; locality and smoothness are assumptions, just not a single global polynomial degree.
:::

## One path, rising flexibility {#sec-smooth-map}

```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 11
%%| fig-height: 2.2
flowchart LR
  line["straight $$\mu$$"] --> local["LOESS"]
  local --> gcv["GCV"]
  gcv --> knots["knots"]
  knots --> penalty["penalty"]
  penalty --> gam["GAM"]
```

Left to right: a straight mean, a local polynomial, a bandwidth chosen by GCV, a spline with knots, a spline with a penalty, then one smooth per covariate.

Each step spends a tuning choice: a bandwidth, a set of knots, or a penalty. The last step is how that choice survives more than one covariate.

::: {.notes}
Walk the Mermaid arrow by arrow; pause on GCV as the first automatic knob, and on GAM as the escape from the curse of dimensionality. Ask which step first stops being a single formula you could write on one line. The map is the spine for the rest of the section.
:::

## Flexibility spends variance {#sec-bias-variance}

At a point $x_0$, the bias–variance decomposition for the curve is:

$$
\mathbb{E}\big(\hat\mu(x_0) - \mu(x_0)\big)^2
  = \mathrm{Var}\big(\hat\mu(x_0)\big)
  + \mathrm{Bias}\big(\hat\mu(x_0)\big)^2.
$$

Predicting a new $Y$ adds $\mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon \mid x_0)$ on top of that. More flexibility usually cuts the bias and raises the variance. A small bandwidth is the noisy end. A large bandwidth is the oversmoothed end.

::: {.notes}
Read the displayed decomposition at $x_0$, then add verbally that irreducible $\mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon \mid x)$ is extra when predicting $Y$. Students often blame "overfitting" only on wiggly $\hat\mu$ and forget the noise term. Tie "small $h$" and "large span" to the two ends of the trade-off named on the slide.
:::

## A kernel is a local bump {#sec-kde}

Before the regression, the same idea estimates a density. Each observation contributes a bump of width $h$:

$$
\hat f_h(x) = \frac{1}{nh}\sum_{i=1}^{n} K\!\left(\frac{x_i - x}{h}\right).
$$

They name four kernels: Gaussian, Epanechnikov, uniform, and triangular. Bandwidth matters far more than which of those four you pick.

::: {.notes}
For a Gaussian kernel, Silverman's rule of thumb is $\hat h \approx 1.06\,\hat\sigma\, n^{-1/5}$. They say it is often adequate for a roughly unimodal symmetric density, and that it fails for a skewed, multimodal, or heavy-tailed density. The AMISE-optimal bandwidth shrinks as $n^{-1/5}$: nonparametric rates are slower than a well-specified parametric rate.
:::
## Local weights for $\mu(x_0)$ {#sec-nw}

::: model
$$
\hat\mu(x_0) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i(x_0)\, Y_i, \qquad \sum_i w_i(x_0) = 1.
$$
Nadaraya–Watson sets $w_i$ from $K((x_i-x_0)/h)$. That fit is a local constant: a weighted average of nearby $Y_i$.
:::

Near the edge of $x$ the window is one-sided, so a local constant is pulled inward and the curve flattens. That boundary bias is why the next fit is local linear.

::: {.notes}
In the model callout, emphasise $\sum_i w_i(x_0)=1$ so $\hat\mu$ is a convex combination of neighbours. At the boundary, name the flattening before students decide the biology "drops off". The next slide is the fix: fit a line in the window instead of a constant.
:::

## Fit a line in the window {#sec-local-linear}

At $x_0$, minimise the weighted residual sum of squares of a line in $(x_i - x_0)$. The fitted value is the local intercept.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Degree 0 is the local constant, Nadaraya–Watson.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} Degree 1 is local linear. The usual default: less boundary bias, still local.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} Degree 2 is local quadratic.

The SIB course LOESS often uses the quadratic. The two sources agree on the algorithm and differ on the default degree.

::: {.notes}
Step the three fragments: 0 recovers Nadaraya–Watson, 1 is the usual compromise, 2 is what LOESS may use. Ask why the fitted value is the intercept at $x_0$ rather than the slope. Mention the SIB versus StatHarbor default only as a software detail, not a statistical fight.
:::

## LOESS is that regression, with a span {#sec-loess}

::: model
LOESS is local polynomial regression with a nearest-neighbour window. The span is a proportion of the sample, in $(0, 1]$. A typical example is $0.3$. The window widens where $x$ is sparse. The usual weight is the tricube kernel.
:::

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Choose the span, find the nearest neighbours, weight them, fit the local polynomial.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} The fit does not start from a named formula, and it does not return one.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} It wants a large, densely sampled $x$. The usual use is exploratory smoothing of a scatterplot.

::: {.notes}
Contrast span $0.3$ with a fixed $h$: neighbours count, so sparse regions get a wider window. Fragment 2 is the honesty check—no single $\beta_0+\beta_1 x$ to publish. Fragment 3 sets expectations: LOESS is for looking, not for a table of coefficients without extra work.
:::

## Robust LOESS reweights large residuals {#sec-loess-robust}

Robust LOESS protects the local fit from a few extreme $Y_i$.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Fit an initial LOESS curve.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} Compute residuals.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} Downweight unusually large residuals.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="4"} Refit with both the proximity weights and those robustness weights.

The span is still chosen separately. The next slide is that choice, for a linear smoother. Robust weights take the fit outside the linear-smoother case their GCV formula assumes.

::: {.notes}
Walk the four fragments as an iteration students may not see in code. Stress that span tuning is separate from robust reweighting. Flag the last sentence: once robust weights enter, the neat $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{Y}$ story on the GCV slide is only approximate.
:::

## GCV chooses the bandwidth {#sec-gcv}

A linear smoother is $\hat{\mathbf{Y}} = \mathbf{S}\mathbf{Y}$. Leave-one-out cross-validation refits $n$ times. Generalised cross-validation approximates it:

$$
\mathrm{GCV}(h)
  = \frac{\frac{1}{n}\sum_i\big(Y_i - \hat\mu(x_i)\big)^2}{\big(1 - \mathrm{tr}(\mathbf{S})/n\big)^2}.
$$

The bandwidth that minimises GCV is their predictive choice. The same criterion, with the penalty weight in place of $h$, is how they tune a smoothing spline. Kernel choice stays secondary.

::: {.notes}
Point at $\mathrm{tr}(\mathbf{S})/n$ in the denominator as the complexity penalty. Ask what happens if $h$ is so small that $\hat\mu(x_i)$ nearly interpolates. Link forward to splines: $\lambda$ replaces $h$ in the same GCV pattern on a later slide.
:::

## A global polynomial moves the whole curve

Extend the line by powers of $x$. A quadratic uses $x$ and $x^2$. Stop at degree 3 or 4: higher powers become strange near the ends of $x$. A global polynomial also means a local change can alter the fit far away.

[Lopez-Ayala, Riley, Collins, and Zimmermann](https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2024-082440) add fractional polynomials: powers that need not be whole numbers. The function is still defined on the whole range of $x$, so a pile of values in one tail can distort the shape everywhere.

::: {.notes}
Use the end-of-range warning: high-degree terms wiggle where data are thin. Fractional polynomials are still global—one tail can bend the entire curve. This slide motivates knots and penalties next; local methods are not the only fix for global rigidity.
:::

## A regression spline spends its flexibility at knots {#sec-reg-spline}

::: model
Knots $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_K$ cut $x$ into intervals. A cubic piece is fitted in each, with $f$, $f'$, and $f''$ continuous at the knots. The truncated-power basis is $\beta_0 + \beta_1 x + \beta_2 x^2 + \beta_3 x^3 + \sum_k \theta_k (x-\xi_k)_+^3$.
:::

The analyst chooses how many knots, and where. A common default is the quantiles of $x$. After the continuity constraints, each added knot costs about one degree of freedom, and the spline is most flexible where the knots are dense. A step function, a piecewise constant, misses the trend unless the cut-points are real breaks. That is the [dichotomisation](#sec-dichotomise) case.

::: {.notes}
In the model callout, read the truncated-power basis once, then say software rarely uses it raw. Knot placement is a scientific choice disguised as a default quantile rule. The dichotomisation link is deliberate: cutting $x$ is a spline with too few pieces and wrong continuity.
:::

## B-splines span the same curves {#sec-b-spline}

The truncated-power basis is clear and numerically unstable. A B-spline basis spans the same spline space with local support: each basis function is zero outside a few intervals. The design matrix is sparse, and the fit is better conditioned. Software prefers this basis. The knots are still chosen by hand. The next smoother removes that choice and penalises roughness instead.

::: {.notes}
Students who implement splines meet instability before they meet the maths. Local support means a knot move affects only a band of basis functions—good for interpretation and for sparse matrices. Hand-chosen knots remain; the penalty slide removes counting knots as the main tuning problem.
:::

## A penalty replaces the knot count {#sec-smooth-spline}

::: model
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\big(Y_i - \mu(x_i)\big)^2
  + \lambda \int \big(\mu''(t)\big)^2\,\mathrm{d}t.
$$
The first term is fidelity. The integral is curvature. Here $\lambda$ is the penalty weight, not a Poisson rate and not a relative intensity. Large $\lambda$ forces a smooth curve. Small $\lambda$ allows a wiggly one. A straight line has $\mu'' = 0$, so the penalty does not forbid a bend. It discourages a bend the data do not support.
:::

::: {.notes}
Read $\lambda$ carefully against Poisson notation from the previous chapter. Large $\lambda$ shrinks curvature; small $\lambda$ chases the data. The straight-line remark prevents the myth that penalised splines cannot be linear—they can, when the data justify it.
:::

## The solution is a natural cubic spline {#sec-spline-theorem}

::: theorem
The minimiser, over a suitable class of functions, is a natural cubic spline with a knot at every observed $x_i$.
:::

The same properties hold: piecewise cubic, knots at the distinct $x_i$, continuous first and second derivatives, and linear beyond the extreme knots. An infinite-dimensional penalty problem becomes a finite spline. In matrix form it is penalised least squares, the ridge pattern $(\mathbf{N}^{\top}\mathbf{N} + \lambda\boldsymbol{\Omega})^{-1}\mathbf{N}^{\top}\mathbf{Y}$.

Effective degrees of freedom are $\mathrm{tr}(\mathbf{S}_\lambda)$. As $\lambda \to \infty$ the fit approaches a straight line. As $\lambda \to 0$ it approaches interpolation. GCV, with $\mathrm{tr}(\mathbf{S}_\lambda)$ in the denominator, chooses $\lambda$.

::: {.notes}
The theorem is the bridge from calculus to computation: one knot per distinct $x_i$, yet a finite solve. Walk $\mathrm{tr}(\mathbf{S}_\lambda)$ as effective df and tie $\lambda\to\infty$ and $\lambda\to 0$ to the bias–variance slide. GCV reuses the same trace in the denominator as for kernel smoothers.
:::

## Restricted cubics are the applied form of that constraint {#sec-rcs}

[The BMJ guide](https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2024-082440) calls the natural spline a restricted cubic spline: cubics between the inner knots, and a straight line before the first knot and after the last. The tails, where points are few and outliers bite, stay stable. With $k$ knots the model spends $k-1$ degrees of freedom. Three knots, their usual start, sit at the 10th, 50th, and 90th percentiles of $x$. Three to five knots is the range they recommend, and AIC can choose the number. The exact quantile locations rarely change the curve.

Coefficients of a spline are not the summary. The partial-effect plot is.

::: {.notes}
Translate "natural" to straight tails beyond the outer knots—where extrapolation would otherwise explode. Three knots at 10th, 50th, 90th percentiles are a concrete default to quote. End on the BMJ line: report partial effects, not raw $\theta_k$ from the basis.
:::

## The curve can rise twice {#sec-rcs-glucose}

On the meningitis data, CSF glucose against bacterial meningitis: a straight line, and a cut at 62 mg/dL, both miss the second rise at high glucose that the three-knot restricted cubic shows.

::: assumption
Do not categorise a continuous covariate, and do not assume a straight line, whether the aim is to predict, explain, or describe. Spend degrees of freedom first on the covariates that matter. Choosing the shape by eye, before the fit, spends phantom degrees of freedom and makes the interval too narrow.
:::

::: {.notes}
Point at 62 mg/dL as the failed dichotomy, then at the second rise only the spline catches. The assumption callout is the policy slide: eyeballing the shape before fitting steals df and narrows intervals dishonestly. About two minutes if you discuss phantom df.
:::

## A neighbourhood empties as dimension grows {#sec-curse}

Local methods need nearby observations. As the number of covariates grows, those neighbourhoods empty, and "local" stops meaning a small window. Naive multivariate smoothing breaks down. The practical compromise is an additive mean: one smooth per covariate, estimated as a one-dimensional problem.

::: {.notes}
Draw an empty ball in $p$ dimensions with fixed neighbour count—intuition beats formula here. Additive structure is a modelling assumption, not a free lunch: no interactions unless you add them. The GAM slide is the constructive response named on this slide.
:::

## A GAM is an additive smooth on the link {#sec-gam}

::: model
$$
g(\mu) = \beta_0 + s_1(x_1) + \cdots + s_p(x_p),
\qquad \mu = \mathbb{E}(Y \mid \mathbf{x}).
$$
A logistic GAM uses the logit. A Poisson GAM uses the log. Each $s_j$ is a one-dimensional smoother. Backfitting cycles through partial residuals until the smooths settle.
:::

Because the mean is a sum, each $\hat s_j$ has its own plot: a line, a threshold, a saturation, a U-shape. That plot is the component, not a black-box importance score. The BMJ guide names `mgcv` for this fit, beside `rms` for the restricted cubic, and treats GAMs and LOESS as the alternatives to fractional polynomials and splines.

::: {.notes}
Read $g(\mu)$ first so Poisson and logistic examples slot in from the GLM hour. Each $\hat s_j$ plot is the estimand for that covariate, holding others in the partial-residual sense. Mention `mgcv` and `rms` as the two R cultures named on the slide.
:::

## Which smoother for which task {#sec-which-smoother}

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Exploratory visualisation: LOESS.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} Density estimation: kernel density estimation.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} A stable smooth regression in one covariate: smoothing splines.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="4"} Moderate flexibility with the basis written down: regression splines, including restricted cubics and B-splines.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="5"} Several covariates, with a plot per covariate: a generalised additive model.

::: {.notes}
Step the five fragments and ask the room to match a CA-SYS or SPRUCE exploratory plot to item 1 versus item 5. KDE is easy to forget because regression stole the hour. Item 4 is when you need a written basis for simulation or reporting.
:::

## Four controls on the flexibility {#sec-smooth-takeaways}

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Smoothness or locality replaces a rigid global formula.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} The control is a bandwidth, a penalty, or the size of the basis.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} Choosing that control is part of the performance, not a detail after the fit.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="4"} In higher dimensions, an additive structure, or some other structure, becomes necessary.

::: {.notes}
StatHarbor's reading list: Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman (2009), *The Elements of Statistical Learning*; Wood (2017), *Generalized Additive Models: An Introduction with R*; Wasserman (2006), *All of Nonparametric Statistics*; Fan and Gijbels (1996), *Local Polynomial Modelling and Its Applications*; Silverman (1986), *Density Estimation for Statistics and Data Analysis*; Green and Silverman (1994), *Nonparametric Regression and Generalized Linear Models*. MARS and projection pursuit are their two extensions that relax strict additivity. They mark the links to kernels and neural nets as conceptual, not equivalences.
:::


# Experimental design {.center}

:abacus: Twenty minutes for the plan. Vocabulary, contrasts, and the longitudinal close are the reference stack.

## The analysis inherits the plan

A mixed model does not repair an undescribed design. Before the formula, name:

- the unit that received the treatment
- the unit that was measured
- what was randomised, blocked, or only observed
- which rows are repeats of the same unit

Carbon data are often hierarchical, multi-site, and repeated in time. The random effect is the translation of that structure, not a decorative term.

::: {.notes}
Open with the headline: the formula cannot fix a plan you never wrote down. Walk the four bullets in order and ask the room to name SPRUCE's treatment unit (the enclosure) and the observational unit on a flux row (collar by date). The common slip is to treat every depth–date line as an independent replicate; that mistake is what the random effect is meant to encode, not to paper over.
:::

## A short map of plans {.smaller}

| Plan | What is randomised or assigned |
| --- | --- |
| Completely randomised | Treatments to units, with no blocking factor |
| Randomised block | Treatments inside a block that soaks up a nuisance gradient |
| Factorial | Two or more factors crossed, so that interactions exist |
| Split-plot or strip-plot | A hard-to-change factor on the large unit, another factor inside it |
| Regression or gradient | A continuous driver, with enough levels to see the shape |
| Nested or hierarchical | Units inside units, when crossing is impossible |

Tanaka's [edibble slides](https://emitanaka.org/slides/toronto2022/) are a precise way to write units and treatments down before you fit anything.

::: {.notes}
Use the table as a menu, not a checklist. Pause on split-plot: the hard-to-change factor is randomised on the large unit (enclosure $\mathrm{CO}_2$), while the easy factor can sit inside (collar schedule). Ask which row best describes CA-SYS tillage zones versus the mixed zone. Tanaka is the homework link for writing units before `lmer()`.
:::

## Completely randomised, then blocked

Completely randomised: every enclosure could have received every warming level, and you trust the site to be homogeneous.

Blocked: you group units that share a nuisance (a boardwalk, a water-table band, a day) and randomise **inside** the block.

SPRUCE's design page says a block was considered, and that preliminary surveys did not show a gradient strong enough to require one. That is a design assumption, and it belongs in the report.

::: {.notes}
Contrast the two paragraphs: CRD needs homogeneity across enclosures; blocking needs a named nuisance gradient inside each block. SPRUCE's "no block" sentence is an assumption about the bog, not a statistical default. Experimental unit for warming assignment remains the enclosure in both layouts.
:::

## Factorial: the interaction is the point

Warming and $\mathrm{CO}_2$ are crossed. The scientific question on the design page is not only "does warming matter?" It is also "does elevated $\mathrm{CO}_2$ **change** the warming response?"

That second question is $\beta_3$ in

$$
g(\mu_{ij}) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1j} + \beta_2 x_{2j} + \beta_3 x_{1j} x_{2j} + \cdots
$$

If you fit two separate slopes and then compare their $p$-values, you have skipped $\beta_3$.

::: {.notes}
Point at $\beta_3$ and the product term $x_{1j}x_{2j}$: that is the "does $\mathrm{CO}_2$ change the warming response?" estimand on the design page. Warming and $\mathrm{CO}_2$ are crossed across enclosures: each enclosure $j$ receives one combination. Students often run two one-factor models; say explicitly that those tests are not the interaction row.
:::

## Split-plot: the large unit is hard to change

A factor that is expensive to reset is applied to the large unit. A factor that is easy to reset is applied inside it.

Cookie-dough batches and oven trays are the textbook version. In the field, an enclosure's $\mathrm{CO}_2$ setpoint and a collar's measurement schedule are not interchangeable units.

The error term for the large-unit factor is the large-unit variance. Using the inner residual will make the interval too small.

::: {.notes}
Cookie dough and oven trays are the anchor image; then map large unit to enclosure setpoint and inner unit to collar measurement. The misread is pooling collar residuals to test a whole-enclosure factor—that uses the wrong denominator. Name which variance belongs on the enclosure factor before you open the mixed-model slide.
:::

## Crossed, nested, or both {#sec-crossed-nested}

```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 10
%%| fig-height: 3.2
flowchart TD
  warming["Warming on $$j$$"]
  co2["CO2 on $$j$$"]
  enclosure["Enclosure $$j$$"]
  collar["Collar"]
  date["Date $$i$$"]
  warming --> enclosure
  co2 --> enclosure
  enclosure --> collar --> date
```

Warming and $\mathrm{CO}_2$ are crossed on the enclosure. Collars and dates are nested in the enclosure. A technical replicate nested in a core is a further level, and it is rarely the level of inference.

::: {.notes}
Trace the Mermaid arrows: warming and $\mathrm{CO}_2$ both hit enclosure $j$; collar and date $i$ sit below. Ask where a core technical replicate would hang, and whether inference should live there. Schielzeth Figure 1 on the next pages is the same vocabulary with different ink.
:::

## A random intercept is a distribution on $u_j$ {#sec-random-intercept}

```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 10
%%| fig-height: 2.6
flowchart LR
  mean["$$g(\mu_{ij}) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_j + u_j$$"]
  cluster["$$u_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2)$$"]
  mean --> cluster
```

Use a Gaussian equation for $Y_{ij}$ only when $Y$ can live on that scale. For a count or a detection, the cluster term sits on the link, as in the diagram.

$u_j$ is a property of the cluster. It is not a covariate you forgot to measure, unless you are willing to say so. Ignoring it is the pitfall [repeated rows are not replicates](#sec-clusters).

::: {.notes}
Read the equation on the link scale in the diagram, not on the count scale unless $Y$ is Gaussian. $u_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2)$ is a distribution over enclosures, not a missing fixed covariate. If someone proposes "just add enclosure as a factor", ask whether they have all levels or a sample of bogs—that leads into the next slide's columns.
:::

## Fixed and random name the target {#sec-fixed-random-target}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
**Fixed**

A contrast you want to report, such as the warming slope.

If you already have every level in the population, a fixed indicator for each level is coherent.
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
**Random**

A level you want to generalise over, such as enclosures drawn from a bog.

Ten enclosures is a small sample of a variance. $\hat\sigma_u$ will be noisy.
:::
::::

Partial pooling is the practical reason to bother: an enclosure with two bad weeks borrows strength from the others. The meta-analysis version, one estimate per site, is [fixed effect or random effects](#sec-fixed-random-meta).

::: {.notes}
Left column: warming slope is a fixed estimand you report. Right column: enclosures are a random draw from a bog population, and ten of them makes $\hat\sigma_u$ noisy—quote that line. Partial pooling is the punchline for sparse clusters. Link forward to the meta-analysis bullet on Huber's slide later in the section.
:::

## Repeated measures are a time series inside $j$

Do not run a separate test at each date and then narrate the dates that were "significant".

A first model for a repeated flux:

- a term for time, or for season
- the treatment, which does not change inside the enclosure
- the treatment $\times$ time interaction, if the contrast itself changes
- a dependence for the residuals, or a random intercept for the enclosure

Dates are $i$. They do not become new experimental units because there are many of them.

::: {.notes}
Warn against the "significant Tuesdays" narrative first. Then walk the bullet list: treatment is constant inside enclosure $j$; interaction matters if warming changes the time course. Index $i$ is the wave, not a new experimental unit. This slide sets up the AR(1) and `(time | enclosure)` calls near the end of the section.
:::

## SPRUCE chose a regression, not five bins

The design page records an earlier incomplete factorial, then a quantitative comparison: more temperature levels, used as a regression, resolved the shape better than a small ANOVA, at lower cost.

What was built:

- five warming levels, from $+0$ to $+9^\circ\mathrm{C}$, steps of $2.25^\circ\mathrm{C}$
- each level at ambient and at elevated $\mathrm{CO}_2$
- ten enclosures, plus chamberless areas

The number of experimental units has to exceed the number of regression parameters you intend to estimate.

::: {.notes}
Stress the design-page trade-off: quantitative regression beat a small ANOVA on cost and shape. Read the warming grid: $+0$ to $+9^\circ\mathrm{C}$ in steps of $2.25^\circ\mathrm{C}$, crossed with ambient versus elevated $\mathrm{CO}_2$, on ten enclosures. Ask how many parameters that grid wants versus $n=10$ enclosures—the next slide's table answers "what is $n$ for which question?"
:::

## Where the replicate is {#sec-replicate}

| If you need… | The replicate is… |
| --- | --- |
| The warming slope | The enclosure, $n = 10$ |
| A collar method variance | Collars within an enclosure |
| A seasonal curve | Dates within an enclosure, with dependence |
| A claim about other bogs | Something this design does not contain |

Pooling every depth-date row into one residual answers none of these cleanly. The SPRUCE rows were introduced as [one line per plot](#sec-spruce-profiles).

::: {.notes}
Read the table row by row. For the warming slope, $n=10$ means ten enclosures, not hundreds of flux rows. Collar and seasonal rows are different estimands. The pool-everything residual is the error students reach for when they paste the long data frame into `lm()`.
:::

## Do not bin the gradient you built {#sec-spruce-bins}

The design page allows a fallback: bin the ten enclosures into low, medium, and high for an ANOVA, if a variable forces it.

That fallback spends the design. Adjacent levels, $+4.5$ and $+6.75$, become either "the same" or "different" by a cut. The curve we already plotted is exactly what the binning throws away.

Keep $x_1$ continuous. Report the slope per $^\circ\mathrm{C}$, with an interval, and show the fitted mean. The same cut, as a reporting pitfall, is [do not dichotomise](#sec-dichotomise).

::: {.notes}
The fallback ANOVA bins are on the design page for software constraints, not for science. Name the adjacent levels $+4.5$ and $+6.75^\circ\mathrm{C}$ that a three-bin cut would glue together. Tie back to the SPRUCE curve already shown in the datasets section—binning throws away exactly that shape.
:::

## A controlled experiment sets the conditions {#sec-controlled}

[Huber, chapter 13](https://www.huber.embl.de/msmb/13-chap.html#sec-design-typesexps): you control the system, the environment, and the readout.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} The vignette is a characterised cell line, a defined medium, a measured dose, and a pathway reporter read at 72 h.

::: {.notes}
Huber chapter 13 is the authority line: control system, environment, and readout. Reveal the fragment and ask what is held fixed at 72 h. This is the contrast anchor before "study" and "trial" loosen different levers.
:::

## A study leaves some conditions free {#sec-study}

In a study, conditions that move the outcome are outside the investigator's control. A field example is the weather, the food, and the predators.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} In an observational study the variable of interest itself is not assigned. That is where the reminder sits: correlation is not causation.

::: {.notes}
Field weather is the uncontrolled nuisance example. Step the fragment: the exposure of interest is not assigned, so the causal section's $do(x)$ language matters later. SPRUCE is not this slide—it is closer to the controlled-experiment and trial pair.
:::

## A trial randomises the assignment {#sec-rct}

A randomised controlled trial still cannot hold every nuisance fixed. It does control who receives the intervention.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} With a large enough sample, the nuisances average out, and the contrast can be read as an effect of that assignment.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} The trial is prospective: the outcome is unknown when the groups are assigned.

::: {.notes}
Fragment one: randomisation controls assignment, not every nuisance. Fragment two: prospective timing—outcome unknown at allocation. Ask whether warming at SPRUCE is "assigned" in this sense (yes on enclosure). Bridge to the causal deck: randomisation is a design fact, not a covariate trick.
:::

## A meta-analysis studies the studies {#sec-meta-analysis}

Huber calls a meta-analysis an observational study of earlier experiments or studies. Two motives: a larger effective sample, and the hope that study-level bias averages out across studies.

The hierarchical version, one estimate per site, is [fixed effect or random effects](#sec-fixed-random-meta).

::: {.notes}
Meta-analysis is observational at study level: you did not randomise which papers exist. The two motives on the slide are sample size and hope that study-level bias averages out—say both, without overselling the second. Point to the link back to fixed versus random effects for "one estimate per site".
:::

## Twenty weighings are not ten people {#sec-tech-bio}

[Huber's weight-loss question](https://www.huber.embl.de/msmb/13-chap.html#biological-versus-technical-replicates). Twenty weighings of one person, on a precise scale, remeasure a value you already know. Ten people, weighed once each, start to ask whether the effect travels.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Name the level you can generalise to: laboratory, operator, machine, protocol, strain, litter, animal. "Technical" and "biological" are too coarse for that list.

::: {.notes}
Huber's twenty-versus-ten weighings is the memorable line. Step the fragment and make students pick a concrete level—sequencing run, core, plot—not the vague label "technical". That list feeds directly into the replicate table for SPRUCE and CA-SYS.
:::

## Randomise the nuisance you cannot block {#sec-randomise}

When the nuisance is unknown in advance, randomisation is the practical strategy: at a large enough sample, its effect averages out. It also limits unconscious bias, such as extra care for the samples that were hard to obtain.

He points to Senn (2004) for the ways a "better" scheme than simple randomisation can go wrong.

::: {.notes}
When blocking is impossible, randomisation is the practical defence—both against unknown nuisances and unconscious handling bias. Mention Senn as the caution on fancy schemes without lecturing the chapter. Ask for one carbon-lab nuisance you could randomise but not block (plate position, run order).
:::

## Balance makes each effect identifiable {#sec-balanced}

A balanced design gives every factor combination the same number of replicates. Each factor's effect can then be separated. A nuisance that is balanced against the factor of interest is in the same position.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} An unbalanced plan can be forced by cost. The warning is that the analysis is then on thin ice.

::: {.notes}
Balanced means equal replication per combination so effects separate. Step the fragment: unbalanced designs are common when money runs out—keep the "thin ice" phrase for the methods section. Do not confuse balance with randomisation; you need both stories in different papers.
:::

## Pairing is the smallest block {#sec-pairing}

[Block what you can, and randomise what you cannot](https://www.huber.embl.de/msmb/13-chap.html#blocking-and-pairing). A paired design is the simplest block: twins, or the same person before and after.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} Fisher criticised Darwin for putting every cross-pollinated plant on the same side of the pot. A coin flip for the side would have kept that side from masquerading as the cross.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} When a natural pair does not exist, a matched design builds pairs on age, sex, or health, and treats one of the pair.

::: {.notes}
Fisher's Darwin pot-side story is the vivid warning—blocking is about removing a directional nuisance. Fragment two: matched pairs when nature does not give you twins. Pairing is the smallest block; SPRUCE blocking discussion earlier is the same idea at enclosure scale.
:::

## Treatment contrasts use one reference {#sec-contr-treatment}

[Larmarange, chapter 26](https://larmarange.github.io/guide-R/analyses/contrastes.html#contrastes-de-type-traitement). For an unordered factor, R's default is `contr.treatment`. A factor with $n$ levels becomes $n-1$ columns. The reference level sits in the intercept.

In the `marker ~ grade` example the intercept is $1.0669$ and the grade II coefficient is $-0.3864$. The mean at grade II is the sum:

$$
1.0669 + (-0.3864) = 0.6805.
$$

`contr.treatment(n, base =)` and `contr.SAS()` move which level is the reference.

::: {.notes}
Walk the arithmetic on the slide: intercept $1.0669$ plus grade II coefficient $-0.3864$ gives $0.6805$. Emphasise $n-1$ columns and one reference in the intercept. Students confuse "coefficient for II" with "mean at II" unless you do the sum aloud. `contr.treatment` and `contr.SAS` only move the reference level.
:::

## Sum contrasts use the mean of the level means {#sec-contr-sum}

[`contr.sum`](https://larmarange.github.io/guide-R/analyses/contrastes.html#contrastes-de-type-somme) compares each level with the unweighted mean of the level means. The last level is coded $-1$, so one factor's coefficients sum to zero and the intercept is that grand mean.

The grade level that was coded $-1$ has deviation $-(0.1525 - 0.2339) = 0.0814$, which is also $0.9958 - 0.9144$.

Fitted values, residual sum of squares, and AIC match the treatment-contrast fit. What changes is the reading of each coefficient.

::: {.notes}
`contr.sum` centres on the unweighted mean of level means; the $-1$ coding line explains the $0.0814$ deviation. Stress that predictions do not move—only the sentences attached to each $\hat\beta$. Ask which contrast you would use if the scientific question is "grade II versus average grade".
:::

## An ordered factor can carry a trend {#sec-contr-poly}

[Further codings in the same chapter](https://larmarange.github.io/guide-R/analyses/contrastes.html#autres-types-de-contrastes).

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} `MASS::contr.sdif` compares each level with the one before it. For grade, II minus I is $-0.39$, the same gap as the treatment contrast against grade I.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} `contr.helmert` compares each new level with the mean of the earlier levels. Keep it for a question that is exactly that comparison.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="3"} `contr.poly` is the default for an ordered factor. The columns are a linear, a quadratic, and a cubic trend. That is the coding for ordered time or dose when the question is the shape, including a bend. The intercept is again the mean of the level means.

::: {.notes}
Step three fragments in order: successive differences (`contr.sdif`), Helmert versus earlier mean, then polynomial trends for ordered dose or time. Tie `contr.sdif`'s $-0.39$ back to the treatment contrast against grade I. Pick the coding to match the estimand, not R's default habit.
:::

## Choose the runs before the measurements {#sec-doe}

A design of experiments picks the combinations you will run, before any $Y$ is recorded. The organising question is the objective, not the historical name of the matrix.

A smaller matrix buys its size by giving something up. The usual sacrifice is aliasing: two effects share one column, so the fit cannot tell them apart. The four families below are the ones the overview treats as the working set: fractional factorials, definitive screening, a response surface, and an optimal design.

NIST's selection chapter is the map: [how do you select an experimental design?](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri33.htm)

::: {.notes}
DOE chooses $x$ combinations before any $Y$ is measured—the objective drives the matrix, not the historical name. Aliasing is the price of a smaller design: two effects share a column. Name the four families on the slide as the working set for the next four slides, then point to NIST as the map.
:::

## A fraction spends the interactions {#sec-doe-fraction}

A full factorial of $k$ two-level factors needs $2^k$ runs. Ten factors need $1024$. A fractional factorial keeps $2^{k-p}$ runs. Five factors at a quarter fraction need $2^{5-2} = 8$, against $32$ for the full $2^5$.

Resolution says which effects are aliased. A low resolution is a screen for main effects. A high resolution leaves the interactions you care about separable. NIST's note on resolution is [here](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri3345.htm).

::: {.notes}
Use the numbers on the slide: $2^{5-2}=8$ runs versus $32$ for a full $2^5$. Resolution vocabulary tells you which interactions are sacrificed—low resolution screens mains; high resolution protects interactions you still need. Students sometimes treat a fraction as "the same factorial but cheaper" without reading the alias table.
:::

## A definitive screen also sees a curve {#sec-doe-dsd}

Plackett–Burman is the classical screen: $11$ two-level factors in $12$ runs, with main effects aliased with two-factor interactions. NIST describes it as a design for when the main effects are the focus.

A definitive screening design is the modern alternative for continuous factors. The run count is about $2k+1$, so $14$ factors need about $29$ runs rather than $2^{14}$. Each factor has levels $-1$, $0$, and $+1$, so a quadratic term $\beta_{11} x^2$ can be seen. JMP's account: main effects are orthogonal to two-factor interactions, and those interactions are not completely aliased with each other. [Definitive screening designs](https://www.jmp.com/en/statistics-knowledge-portal/design-of-experiments/screening-designs/definitive-screening-designs).

::: {.notes}
Contrast Plackett–Burman's $11$ factors in $12$ runs with main effects aliased to two-factor interactions against definitive screening's three levels per factor. The $0$ level is what lets a quadratic $\beta_{11}x^2$ appear—Plackett–Burman cannot do that. Quote $2k+1$ when someone asks how many runs for fourteen factors.
:::

## A surface needs three levels {#sec-doe-rsm}

Once a few factors remain, the question moves from "which factor?" to "where is the optimum?". The working model is quadratic: a linear term, a square, and a product for each pair.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="1"} A central composite design is a factorial, plus axial points $(\pm\alpha, 0)$ and $(0, \pm\alpha)$, plus centre points.

[]{.fragment fragment-index="2"} A Box–Behnken design also estimates that quadratic surface, and it avoids the corner where every factor is at its extreme.

Both are in JMP's [introduction to DOE](https://www.jmp.com/support/help/en/19.2/jmp/introduction-to-doe.shtml).

::: {.notes}
After screening, the question is "where is the optimum?"—hence linear, square, and product terms. Fragment one: central composite adds axial and centre points. Fragment two: Box–Behnken avoids the all-extremes corner. Both need more than two levels per factor; link back to SPRUCE's continuous warming grid as a field analogue of "shape", not necessarily a CCD.
:::

## An optimal design is computed for the constraint {#sec-doe-optimal}

D-optimal, I-optimal, and A-optimal designs are algorithm-built matrices. They are the modern choice when the run budget is tight, the factors are a mix of continuous and categorical, or the experimental region is an awkward shape.

The matrix is less readable than a textbook factorial. The criterion is explicit: D-optimality, for example, targets the precision of the coefficient estimates. A mixture, where the factors are parts of a constant sum, needs its own geometry rather than one of these factorials.

::: {.notes}
When the region is awkward or the budget is tight, the computer builds the matrix against D-, I-, or A-optimality. Say explicitly that the criterion replaces the pretty symmetry of a textbook factorial. Mixture designs are the exception named on the slide—proportions summing to one are not a standard factorial cube.
:::

## A decision tree for the matrix {#sec-doe-tree}

- **Objective**, chosen before any measurement
  - **Screen**, when many factors might matter: a fractional factorial, Plackett–Burman, or a definitive screening design
    - **Interactions**, once a few factors remain: a full or high-resolution factorial
      - **Optimise**: a central composite, a Box–Behnken, or an optimal design
  - **Taguchi**, when the target is robustness to uncontrolled noise
  - **Mixture**, when the factors are parts of a constant sum

::: {.notes}
Walk the nested bullets top-down: objective before measurement, then screen versus optimise branches. Taguchi and mixture are leaves students may never use but should recognise on a methods form. This tree is the thirty-second recap before Schielzeth's figures make crossing and nesting visual.
:::

## Nesting is a property of the experiment {#sec-nested-crossed}

[Schielzeth and Nakagawa](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2041-210x.2012.00251.x) (*Methods in Ecology and Evolution*, 2013). A nested factor can be fitted with the syntax of a crossed factor, provided each nested level has a unique label. The design still decides what the variances mean.

The difference that matters is the interaction variance. In a nested experiment it is pooled with the main-effect variance of the nested factor. A crossed experiment can separate the two. The pooling is fixed by which combinations occur, not by the formula.

If a cross would have been feasible and meaningful, the data are nested by design, and the pooling has to be stated. If a cross would be irrelevant, the nesting is natural.

::: {.notes}
Schielzeth and Nakagawa: unique labels let nested factors use crossed syntax, but the experiment still decides what variances mean. The interaction variance pools with the nested main effect in a nested design; a cross can separate them. Ask whether enclosure-by-collar should be crossed or nested given how SPRUCE was built—the Mermaid slide earlier is your answer key.
:::

## Crossed, nested, blocked, split {#sec-schielzeth-fig1}

![Four layouts: crossed, nested, randomised block, and split-plot. Schielzeth and Nakagawa, Figure 1.](figures/sources/schielzeth-fig1-designs.png){width="96%" fig-alt="Four schematics. Crossed: each level of one factor meets several levels of the other. Nested: each lower level belongs to one upper level. Randomised block: each treatment appears in each block. Split-plot: one treatment on the large unit, another inside it."}

Schielzeth and Nakagawa, Figure 1. Solid lines are a partial cross. Dashed lines complete it. The SPRUCE reading of the same picture is [crossed, nested, or both](#sec-crossed-nested).

::: {.notes}
Four panels: crossed, nested, randomised block, split-plot. Point to solid versus dashed lines in the caption—partial versus completed cross. Map split-plot to large-unit versus within-unit factors without re-deriving the cookie-dough story. Link back to [#sec-crossed-nested] for the SPRUCE labels on the same picture.
:::

## Four variances, if the design allows them {.fit-fig #sec-schielzeth-fig2}

![Four sources of a trait value, and the four variances a crossed design can estimate. Schielzeth and Nakagawa, Figure 2.](figures/sources/schielzeth-fig2-variances.png){width="92%" fig-alt="A flowchart from two factors, their interaction, and other variation, into a trait value, then down to a main-effect variance for each factor, an interaction variance, and a residual variance."}

A crossed design can estimate each of those four variances. Nested sampling leaves the interaction inside the nested factor's variance.

::: {.notes}
Figure 2 flows from two factors and their interaction into trait value, then down to four variance components. In a cross you can estimate interaction variance separately; nested sampling pools it. Students often fit `(1|nested)` and interpret $\sigma_u^2$ as "noise" without stating what was pooled.
:::

## Repeated in time is longitudinal {#sec-longitudinal}

[Angelino and Marcone](https://www.sib.swiss/training/course/20260907_ADDMG), September 2026: a repeated-measures design records each individual more than once. The design is longitudinal when those repeats are ordered in time.

Three predictors sit beside $Y$: time, a covariate that changes with time, and a covariate that does not. The aim is how $\mu$ depends on them across the waves.

Godechot's panel is the econometric name for the same layout: several dates for one person, set against a single cross-section. Within-person changes become visible, and age can be separated from cohort.

::: {.notes}
Angelino and Marcone: repeated measures becomes longitudinal when time is ordered. Name the three predictor types beside $Y$—time, time-varying covariate, time-invariant covariate. Godechot's panel vocabulary helps economists in the room; enclosure waves are the carbon analogue.
:::

## Classical repeated-measures ANOVA wants a rectangle {#sec-sphericity}

The same course lists two limits. The ANOVA keeps only people with every wave. It also assumes sphericity: every pair of times has the same variance of the difference. When that fails, the $F$ ratios inflate.

A mixed model keeps an incomplete person. The dependence goes into $u_j$, or into a residual correlation. Dates stay index $i$. They do not become new experimental units.

::: {.notes}
Two limits of classical RM-ANOVA: complete cases only, and sphericity of differenced times. When sphericity fails, $F$ ratios inflate. Mixed models keep partial follow-up and put dependence in $u_j$ or correlated residuals—preview `corAR1` on the `nlme` slide. Dates remain index $i$, not new units.
:::

## Fixed levels are the ones you would use again {#sec-choose-fixed-random}

A fixed factor has levels you chose in order to compare them, and you would use those levels again. Sex, in their example, is fixed. It shifts the mean of $Y$.

A random factor has levels you can treat as a sample from a population of effects. They need not reappear. The target is their variance, and the usual model for that draw is Gaussian. The same factor can be either, depending on the question. The earlier statement is [fixed and random name the target](#sec-fixed-random-target).

Godechot's condition for a random person effect $a_i$: it is uncorrelated with the covariates. When it is not, he centres each person, the within estimator, and treats $a_i$ as fixed. His Hausman rule follows the comparison: if the within fit and the random-effect fit disagree, keep the within fit.

::: {.notes}
Sex as a fixed factor shifts the mean; random enclosures target $\sigma_u^2$. The same factor can flip roles with the question—link [#sec-fixed-random-target]. Godechot's Hausman story: if $a_i$ correlates with covariates, centre within person and prefer the within fit when it disagrees with the random-effects fit.
:::

## The line is allowed to vary inside $j$ {#sec-multilevel}

::: model
$$
Y_{ij} = (\beta_0 + u_{0j}) + (\beta_1 + u_{1j})\, t_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij}.
$$
:::

$j$ is the person, the child, or the enclosure. $i$ is the wave. $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$ are the mean intercept and the mean slope. $u_{0j}$ and $u_{1j}$ are the cluster's departures, mean zero, usually Gaussian.

The sketch replaces a separate line for each of sixteen children with these four parameters. Ignoring $j$ treats the waves as independent, and the interval uses the wrong variance. The same hierarchy is what lets a longitudinal sample be a multilevel model.

::: {.notes}
Read the model callout: random intercept $u_{0j}$ and random slope $u_{1j}$ on time inside cluster $j$. Sixteen separate lines become four population parameters plus cluster departures. Ignoring $j$ is independence across waves—the wrong standard error. This is the generative story behind the spaghetti plot two slides ahead.
:::

